WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.934
-You know, Al, I'm so psyched
you're on the show.

00:00:02.934 --> 00:00:06.400
Logan, welcome back to the show.
Al, we've never met.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.033
-No.
-But people people have always

00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:10.300
asked me to do
an impression of you,

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.801
and I don't really
have a great one.

00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:14.167
No, I'm not gonna do it.
I promise.

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:16.267
[ Laughter ]
-No, it's cool, it's cool.

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:17.734
-I don't have a great one of you

00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:19.601
because I'm such
a fan of your work.

00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:22.434
But your voice has changed
all throughout your career,

00:00:22.434 --> 00:00:24.067
and you've done
different characters.

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:26.067
-Yes.
-Scarface and, you know,

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:27.734
"Scent of a Woman."
So I don't know which one to do,

00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:30.601
so when I do an impression,
it's all confused,

00:00:30.601 --> 00:00:32.834
and it sounds nothing like you.

00:00:32.834 --> 00:00:36.868
-It's sort of a montage?
-Yeah, it's -- [ Laughs ]

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:40.334
Tony Montage is what I call it,
yeah.

00:00:40.334 --> 00:00:42.701
Yeah.
I'm happy that you're here.

00:00:42.701 --> 00:00:44.667
And, Logan,
I'm happy that you're back.

00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:46.834
-I'm so happy to be here.
-Last time you were on the show,

00:00:46.834 --> 00:00:49.400
I gave you a --
I give you a gift.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.267
-A thermometer.
Your "SNL" thermometer.

00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:53.834
It's like the prized possession
in my house.

00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:55.434
I have it in my --
my -- my kitchen.

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:58.868
It's like the thing
I show people when they come in.

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:01.000
-I didn't believe this,
but then you brought a photo.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.934
This is the thermometer
that I gave to you.

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:05.067
-Oh, yeah.
-From "Saturday Night Live."

00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:07.567
-Yeah, it's right
on the cookbooks.

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:10.367
Oh, yeah.
All right.

00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:12.200
-I'm very thankful
that you kept that.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.400
-Are you kidding?
It was an honor.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.868
I mean, that's so cool.
And not to mention,

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:16.701
I'm, like, a huge "SNL" fan.

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:20.400
So to have this piece of history
from "SNL," from you,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.067
it just meant a lot.
I love it.

00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:24.701
-Logan, you and Al have gotten
to know each other

00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:27.067
doing this series together.

00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:29.634
And Al invited you over
to a poker night

00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:32.300
over at his --
-Yes. Poker.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.367
-When was that?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:36.167
-Pre-pandemic.
It was before --

00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:37.801
-Pre-pandemic.
-Yeah, it was a while ago.

00:01:37.801 --> 00:01:40.067
It was before --
What is that like playing --

00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:42.434
Are you a big poker player, Al?

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:45.067
-Well, I never thought I was,
but perhaps I am.

00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:47.000
-Yeah, wow.
-Well, I was nervous, though,

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.968
to play with you 'cause
I thought you would be amazing,

00:01:48.968 --> 00:01:50.400
and, like, all your friends
are incredible,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.167
and I didn't really know
how to play.

00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:56.601
-Oh, yeah, but you see,

00:01:56.601 --> 00:01:58.934
the games that we used to have,

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:01.434
very low stakes, you know, so...

00:02:01.434 --> 00:02:04.267
But they were because we had
all kinds of people in the game.

00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:06.234
People who worked,
people who didn't.

00:02:06.234 --> 00:02:08.801
So it was it was a very --

00:02:08.801 --> 00:02:10.801
-It's just story time.
And, like, I just remember

00:02:10.801 --> 00:02:13.234
there was, like,
a whole table of pastrami.

00:02:13.234 --> 00:02:15.934
-Oh, pastrami.
-Pastrami and stories,

00:02:15.934 --> 00:02:17.300
and that was it.
-I was like, "Okay.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.567
It's a safe space.
We can play."

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.000
-Yeah.
-Yeah, it was great.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.634
-You have your old faction,
and you have your young faction.

00:02:24.634 --> 00:02:26.067
-But do you have a thing?
Do you have --

00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:28.000
Are you good bluffer?
Are you a good poker --

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.467
-No, I'm not.
I don't -- I don't --

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:33.367
You don't need to bluff when
it's so low, the stakes.

00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:35.300
-Yeah.
-You just go in and go...

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.834
-Just go and just play.
-Yeah, you just play.

00:02:37.834 --> 00:02:39.300
-Just play, yeah.
-Hang out.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.601
-But I used to do
one thing there, I remember,

00:02:42.601 --> 00:02:45.734
which people
will either find funny,

00:02:45.734 --> 00:02:48.367
but the people who I was playing
with didn't find it funny,

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:52.000
but I found it funny because
there were a lot of old friends,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.434
friends I've known
since I was a teenager,

00:02:54.434 --> 00:02:55.801
actually, some of them.

00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:59.767
And what I would do is,
in the middle of a game,

00:02:59.767 --> 00:03:02.133
I just like play cards
because it's so much fun.

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:04.901
The games -- we used to mix up
the games, different games.

00:03:04.901 --> 00:03:06.901
Yeah, we had Texas Hold'em,

00:03:06.901 --> 00:03:09.834
you had Omaha
and all kinds of games.

00:03:09.834 --> 00:03:11.968
So I would just get up
and go into my bathroom

00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:14.767
because it's in my office
we would play,

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:17.400
and I'd look in the mirror

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.968
and there were the old guys
who've known me all my life,

00:03:19.968 --> 00:03:21.567
and I just would...
[ Laughs ]

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:23.400
[ Laughter ]

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.000
I would just yell out --

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.934
I would just yell out

00:03:26.934 --> 00:03:30.267
as I looked in the mirror,
"I'm beautiful!"

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:31.434
[ Laughter ]

00:03:31.434 --> 00:03:34.267
"Why didn't you tell me?!"

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:36.033
-[ Laughing ] What?
And I would think

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:37.801
they would hear it
in the other room, you know?

00:03:37.801 --> 00:03:40.701
-Everyone's just confused, like,
"What is going on in there?"

00:03:40.701 --> 00:03:43.234
-Except for Marty Bregman,

00:03:43.234 --> 00:03:45.868
who is my producer
and knew me a long time,

00:03:45.868 --> 00:03:48.834
he would say,
"He's being funny, you know?"

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:51.200
-Yeah. That's never good when
you have to explain to everyone

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.968
that you're being funny.
I love it.

00:03:52.968 --> 00:03:55.300
"I'm beautiful.
Why didn't you tell me?"

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.300
-I'm so glad
you people are like it.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.934
-Oh, yeah, I thought that was --
I'm gonna steal that bit.

00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:01.167
I'm gonna steal that bit.
I like it.

00:04:01.167 --> 00:04:03.100
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.801
I'm gonna do it tonight.

00:04:04.801 --> 00:04:06.734
-I have a couple of others.
-Yeah.

00:04:06.734 --> 00:04:08.667
-Which I won't go into now,
but you probably --

00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:10.501
we'll probably
get there somehow.

00:04:10.501 --> 00:04:14.634
-I was reading about you,
and before you got into --

00:04:14.634 --> 00:04:17.467
This is probably in the '60s,
your career took off.

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:19.834
You were writing sketch comedy
and doing...

00:04:19.834 --> 00:04:23.367
-Actually, I was.
I was doing -- I had a partner

00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:25.667
who has since gone,

00:04:25.667 --> 00:04:29.968
but when we were young --
we were like 19, 20 years old --

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:33.100
we would get together
and do these skits together,

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.601
and I guess they were
a way of just --

00:04:36.601 --> 00:04:40.000
And then we went further,
and we were, like, partners,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.267
but Second City,
he went with Second City

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:44.300
way back then.
He was quite good.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.467
And Brucie Cohen. He's gone,
but he was quite good.

00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:50.033
And I remained --

00:04:50.033 --> 00:04:51.767
I liked doing him
because, you know,

00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:55.400
I was always so depressed
that, by getting up

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.334
and bringing it out like that,
I'd feel much better.

00:04:58.334 --> 00:05:00.234
-Just make people laugh, yeah.
-And it was nice

00:05:00.234 --> 00:05:02.501
to have an audience,
a live audience.

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:04.801
That's why I always keep
coming back to the theater.

00:05:04.801 --> 00:05:07.133
If you know
a little bit about me, I do --

00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:10.000
I do movies, and I do theater.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.033
-Yeah.
-And because --

00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:14.534
I have a story from that.

00:05:14.534 --> 00:05:16.234
I was trying
to move this to that

00:05:16.234 --> 00:05:18.501
because I got a nice story.
-Please.

00:05:18.501 --> 00:05:20.334
-Yeah.
-We all want to hear it.

00:05:20.334 --> 00:05:21.901
-I don't know
if you've heard this.

00:05:21.901 --> 00:05:24.601
I know --
I know Jimmy hasn't,

00:05:24.601 --> 00:05:27.400
and I don't think
any of you have, really.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.400
Well, I was a young actor
working in Boston,

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.634
and I -- and I was in this play,

00:05:32.634 --> 00:05:34.701
"The Basic Training
of Pavlo Hummel,"

00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:35.834
if you've ever heard of it.
-Yes.

00:05:35.834 --> 00:05:38.567
-David Rabe
is a great playwright.

00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:41.000
and we were in
Boston performing,

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.300
and I remember
it was a long play,

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:45.834
and I had the lead in it,

00:05:45.834 --> 00:05:51.400
and I was playing the play,
and I was out there for a while.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:55.467
And then I noticed
that one part of the audience,

00:05:55.467 --> 00:05:56.934
as though
I was looking out here,

00:05:56.934 --> 00:05:58.601
you know,
I was playing the part,

00:05:58.601 --> 00:06:04.300
but I was feeling someone's
eyes, like, riveted on me.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.934
And it was the first time
that ever happened to me.

00:06:06.934 --> 00:06:10.033
So I was just, like, stunned,
and I thought, "What is this?"

00:06:10.033 --> 00:06:12.467
And I started doing
the performance

00:06:12.467 --> 00:06:14.901
for that part of the audience,

00:06:14.901 --> 00:06:16.968
and I just kept going
and doing it.

00:06:16.968 --> 00:06:19.901
And I thought, "Oh, wow.
I've got to see these eyes,"

00:06:19.901 --> 00:06:20.968
you know?
And I --

00:06:20.968 --> 00:06:22.634
You felt --
You felt a connection.

00:06:22.634 --> 00:06:25.234
Yeah. This is too much.
A huge connection.

00:06:25.234 --> 00:06:28.834
It was, like, overpowering me.
I mean, it was so steady.

00:06:28.834 --> 00:06:31.801
So I thought,
finally curtain call comes,

00:06:31.801 --> 00:06:33.033
so I'm gonna come out,

00:06:33.033 --> 00:06:35.367
and I am gonna look over
in that corner.

00:06:35.367 --> 00:06:38.334
So curtain call comes,
and I go out,

00:06:38.334 --> 00:06:42.100
and that's the first thing I do.
I go right to that corner,

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:43.801
and I see that there's

00:06:43.801 --> 00:06:46.901
two seeing-eye dogs
right in the audience.

00:06:46.901 --> 00:06:49.868
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.868 --> 00:06:52.567
That's a -- that's a fact.
-Wow.

00:06:52.567 --> 00:06:54.167
-That's a true story.

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:56.801
-You felt the connection.
-I felt that --

00:06:56.801 --> 00:06:58.300
Well, they're so riveted,
you know?

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.868
-Yeah, yeah.
-Part of their thing, you know?

00:07:00.868 --> 00:07:02.501
That's what they do.

00:07:02.501 --> 00:07:04.567
And it was amazing to me.
-Yeah.

00:07:04.567 --> 00:07:06.167
-And then I thought,
"That's what theater is."

00:07:06.167 --> 00:07:08.701
You know, that's theater.
-The mystique.

00:07:08.701 --> 00:07:11.534
-Yeah.
A lot of things happen.

00:07:11.534 --> 00:07:14.067
-Let's talk about the second
and final season of "Hunters."

00:07:14.067 --> 00:07:18.934
Logan, you signed on to do this
show before Al was attached.

00:07:18.934 --> 00:07:21.300
How does it feel to have
your co-star be Al Pacino?

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.567
-Oh, my God.
Can you --

00:07:22.567 --> 00:07:25.033
I mean, come on.
It's incredible.

00:07:25.033 --> 00:07:27.200
It's, like, the greatest

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.467
that's ever done it.
The best actor ever.

00:07:30.467 --> 00:07:33.734
And I just was so excited
when I found out.

00:07:33.734 --> 00:07:35.701
And to be able to spend
so much time with you,

00:07:35.701 --> 00:07:37.901
it's been one of the greatest
joys,

00:07:37.901 --> 00:07:39.868
experiences, everything,
in my life.

00:07:39.868 --> 00:07:41.467
I learned so much,
and I love you so much.

00:07:41.467 --> 00:07:42.868
But as you could see,

00:07:42.868 --> 00:07:46.734
I mean, there's -- your trove
of incredible stories.

00:07:46.734 --> 00:07:50.501
And I just -- I can sit and talk
with you for hours and hours.

00:07:50.501 --> 00:07:52.501
-He asked me
about coming on the show.

00:07:52.501 --> 00:07:54.968
I said, "Well, you know,
I've been around for a while,

00:07:54.968 --> 00:07:57.067
so I got a lot of go-tos."

00:07:57.067 --> 00:08:00.300
You know, you come up with the
audience, come up with anything,

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.133
and I'll just --
No, don't.

00:08:02.133 --> 00:08:03.901
[ Laughter ]

00:08:03.901 --> 00:08:06.534
But anything.
I got a story for everything.

00:08:06.534 --> 00:08:11.834
-Logan, you got -- you shot this
show, the series, in Prague.

00:08:11.834 --> 00:08:13.701
And when you were there,
there was another star

00:08:13.701 --> 00:08:15.367
staying at your hotel,

00:08:15.367 --> 00:08:17.234
and I was just wondering
if you could tell that story.

00:08:17.234 --> 00:08:18.667
-Oh, yeah, sure.
I'm like,

00:08:18.667 --> 00:08:20.000
"Why does anybody
want to hear a story from me?

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.801
Turn off my mic.
I want to --"

00:08:21.801 --> 00:08:22.868
-Oh, no!
-I'm sitting here,

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:25.000
I'm captivated.
I want to hear you.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.634
Yeah, we were
shooting in Prague,

00:08:27.634 --> 00:08:30.734
and they had another production.
This is so funny.

00:08:30.734 --> 00:08:34.300
Like, my story is, like,
so not as interesting.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.567
But Chris Hemsworth was
staying there, and he's filming.

00:08:37.567 --> 00:08:39.567
-Who's he?
-He's an actor.

00:08:39.567 --> 00:08:41.801
He's like a guy, a big guy.
-I know, I know.

00:08:41.801 --> 00:08:43.901
-Big swollen dude.

00:08:43.901 --> 00:08:46.567
But, basically, they're doing
like "Extraction 2,"

00:08:46.567 --> 00:08:48.801
and we're filming out there.
This is an action show,

00:08:48.801 --> 00:08:50.100
so you have to do -- you know,

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:51.667
to continue working out
and things like that.

00:08:51.667 --> 00:08:53.567
So I'd always go to the gym,
and I'd be stuck,

00:08:53.567 --> 00:08:55.734
just me and Chris Hemsworth.

00:08:55.734 --> 00:08:57.901
And there's nothing
more emasculating

00:08:57.901 --> 00:08:59.934
than working out
next to Chris Hemsworth.

00:08:59.934 --> 00:09:01.334
-He's -- He's --
Yeah, he's built, he's built.

00:09:01.334 --> 00:09:05.467
-And mind you, mind you,
he has no idea who I am.

00:09:05.467 --> 00:09:08.000
And we're down there often,
and we start talking.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.334
And for some reason he thought
I was a stunt guy on his movie.

00:09:12.334 --> 00:09:14.067
And so --
-But here's the best part.

00:09:14.067 --> 00:09:16.000
-I never -- I never told him
that I wasn't,

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.501
so I just, like, told -- like,

00:09:17.501 --> 00:09:20.000
pretended like I was just
the stunt guy,

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.834
you know, on his film, like,
showing up every day.

00:09:21.834 --> 00:09:23.367
I'm like,
"Yeah, work was crazy yesterday

00:09:23.367 --> 00:09:26.067
in the prison set.
Yeah, for sure. Definitely.

00:09:26.067 --> 00:09:27.834
I know what you mean.
-To this day, does he know...?

00:09:27.834 --> 00:09:29.033
-He probably doesn't know
who I am.

00:09:29.033 --> 00:09:31.234
He has no idea.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:31.234 --> 00:09:34.567
-Chris, if you're watching,
this is Logan Lerman.

00:09:34.567 --> 00:09:36.601
A very talented actor.
You're gonna like his stuff.

00:09:36.601 --> 00:09:38.834
-In Prague.
-He's a very talented actor.

00:09:38.834 --> 00:09:40.767
-But that was a funny --
funny little thing happened

00:09:40.767 --> 00:09:42.801
when we were
filming the second season.

00:09:42.801 --> 00:09:44.167
-Logan, can you set up
what "Hunters" --

00:09:44.167 --> 00:09:45.467
What's going on
this final season?

00:09:45.467 --> 00:09:47.100
-Oh, well, yeah,
this final season,

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:52.167
it plays on the conspiracy that
Hitler survived World War II

00:09:52.167 --> 00:09:56.501
and that he was actually
living in South America.

00:09:56.501 --> 00:10:00.100
And so our team of hunters
are gathering,

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:01.300
you know,
are getting back together

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.167
to try to track him down

00:10:04.167 --> 00:10:07.367
in South America somewhere
and bring him to justice.

00:10:07.367 --> 00:10:08.767
-Here's Al Pacino
and Logan Lerman

00:10:08.767 --> 00:10:10.501
in season one finale
of "Hunters."

00:10:10.501 --> 00:10:11.767
Take a look.

00:10:11.767 --> 00:10:13.834
-Who am I?
If I don't do this --

00:10:13.834 --> 00:10:17.067
if I don't do this, who am I?

00:10:17.067 --> 00:10:18.300
Who?
-You're a boy.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:19.501
That's who you are.

00:10:19.501 --> 00:10:22.167
You didn't even
bring me The Wolf.

00:10:22.167 --> 00:10:23.467
You brought me a [bleep]...

00:10:23.467 --> 00:10:25.834
-I [bleep] found you.
I found you.

00:10:25.834 --> 00:10:29.267
-Jonah, don't let this man
destroy your life, too.

00:10:29.267 --> 00:10:31.734
-What are you gonna do?
-You destroyed my [bleep] world.

00:10:31.734 --> 00:10:33.133
-I destroyed your world?

00:10:33.133 --> 00:10:36.067
There's a [bleep] kid
running around the streets.

00:10:36.067 --> 00:10:38.567
I gave you the world.
What are you doing?

00:10:38.567 --> 00:10:40.033
You're gonna do something
with that?

00:10:40.033 --> 00:10:42.734
-Jonah, he wants you to kill.
Don't give him what he wants.

00:10:42.734 --> 00:10:45.400
-You think I want to live?
Go ahead.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:46.968
Only the hunt lives.

00:10:46.968 --> 00:10:50.000
♪♪

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.801
-Jonah, no, pease.
-Do you understand?

00:10:51.801 --> 00:10:53.801
Only the hunt.

00:10:53.801 --> 00:10:55.634
-[ Speaking foreign language ]

00:10:55.634 --> 00:10:58.400
-You know there'll be
another time for you to do this.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:03.133
You can't do
what you think is necessary.

00:11:03.133 --> 00:11:04.300
-Amen.
[ Gunshot ]

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:05.534
-Aah!

00:11:05.534 --> 00:11:10.200
-Ooh!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.801
Al Pacino and Logan Lerman,
everybody!

00:11:12.801 --> 00:11:15.434
"Hunters," season two,
begins streaming

00:11:15.434 --> 00:11:17.634
on Prime Video this Friday.

00:11:17.634 --> 00:11:19.300
We'll be right back
with more "Tonight Show."

